p consumers  make these better decisions there's also  a ton of textual educational information  behind here that talks about like what  are points and how should you include  these these in your your decision  factors and sorry I'm trying to get back  to the  I think next time I won't toggle between  okay and finally I'll talk about data  that we use for enforcing so there are  bad actors out there in the world that  take advantage of either loopholes or  consumers who are not you know as  understanding of what they are of they  may need to know and there's also times  when people when corporations don't  necessarily know that they're not  complying with all the rules and  regulations so this is an example where  we are data scientists helped out with  this particular enforcement action  because debt collectors are not allowed  to call you at three o'clock in the  morning every night there's rules around  debt collection and so our data  scientists the step that they had to  take was there was a lot of complaints i  believe and there was a reason to  believe that debt collection was being  done inappropriately so when we went in  investigated this there was there was  several things we investigated for but  one of them was was calls in the middle  of the night for people who had  outstanding debt you can't just assume  that because someone's area code on  their phone actually indicates where  they live especially now with cell  phones and companies don't always have  people's addresses so we had to figure  out how could we normalize where do  people live so we took a bunch of  information and then normalized where  the zones were that people were living  to be able to do this study to see was  there a problem with the debt collection  and you can see here that we did  discover a number of of problems with  the the student loan servicing practices  and we got money back for consumers  again so  summarize and i see i'm almost at a time  that we use data to educate we